Name: matchAllChunks

Type: function

Syntax: matchAllChunks(<string>, <regularExpression>)

Summary:
<return|Returns> an <array> of the character positions of every match of
a <regular expression> in the specified <string>.

Introduced: 0.9.16

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
local tMatches
put matchAllChunks("a1 b22 c333", "[a-z]([0-9]+)") into tMatches
-- tMatches[2][0]["start"] is 4, tMatches[2][0]["end"] is 6    (b22)
-- tMatches[2][1]["start"] is 5, tMatches[2][1]["end"] is 6    (22)

Example:
-- Colour every number in a field red
local tMatches, tMatch
put matchAllChunks(the text of field "Report", "[0-9]+") into tMatches
repeat for each element tMatch in tMatches
   set the textColor of char tMatch[0]["start"] to tMatch[0]["end"] \
         of field "Report" to "red"
end repeat

Parameters:
string (string):
The text to search.

regularExpression:
Any expression that evaluates to a regular expression.

Returns (array):
The <matchAllChunks> <function> returns a numerically keyed <array> with
one element for each match, in the order the matches occur in the
<string>. Each element is itself an <array>: key 0 describes the whole
match, key 1 the first parenthesized group, key 2 the second, and so on.
Each of those is an <array> with two keys, "start" and "end", holding
the positions of the first and last characters of the matched text. If
nothing matches, the <function> returns empty.

Description:
Use the <matchAllChunks> <function> to find where every match of a
pattern lies in a <string>. It is to the <matchChunk> <function> what
the <matchAll> <function> is to <matchText>: it finds every match rather
than only the first, and reports character positions rather than the
matched text.

The positions are those a char <chunk> expression uses, so

    char tMatch[0]["start"] to tMatch[0]["end"] of tString

is the text of the match.

Every parenthesized group of the <regularExpression> has a key in every
match. For a group that took no part in a particular match, such as an
optional group, "start" and "end" are both empty. A named group, written
"(?&lt;name&gt;...)", is stored under its name as well as under its number.

For a match of no characters, "end" is one less than "start", and
"start" is the position of the character the match lies in front of.

The matches found, the way the search moves on after each one, the
handling of case and of Unicode, and the errors thrown are the same as
for the <matchAll> <function>.

>*Note:* At most 49 parenthesized groups are returned for each match.

References: filter (command), matchAll (function),
matchChunk (function), matchText (function), replaceText (function),
splitText (function), array (glossary), chunk (glossary),
regular expression (glossary), return (glossary), function (glossary),
string (keyword)
